Therapist: Hi Marcus, come on in. How have things been since we last talked?
Client: About the same, honestly. Maybe a little worse. The chest tightness came back twice this week.
Therapist: Can you walk me through one of those times?
Client: Sure. Tuesday night, I was just sitting on the couch watching TV, nothing was even going on, and out of nowhere my heart started racing and my chest felt tight. Took maybe twenty minutes to pass.
Therapist: Did anything happen right before it started, even something small?
Client: I'd just gotten an email from my landlord about the lease renewal. I didn't think it bothered me, but maybe it did.
Therapist: That's a good catch — sometimes the trigger doesn't register consciously until we go back and look. How many times has that happened this week total?
Client: Twice. Tuesday and then again Friday morning, before a client call.
Therapist: And when you saw Dr. Alvarez last month for your physical, did she have any updated thoughts on the anxiety piece?
Client: She said it lines up with the generalized anxiety diagnosis from a couple years back, and that the physical symptoms are consistent with that, not a heart problem. She wanted me to keep working with you on it before considering medication again.
Therapist: That's helpful context, thank you. How did you feel hearing her say that?
Client: Relieved, mostly. Some part of me is always scared it's something actually wrong with my heart, even though the tests keep coming back clean.
Therapist: That fear is really common with panic-adjacent anxiety — the body's alarm system doesn't know the difference between "something is actually wrong" and "I'm anxious," so it produces the same physical signals either way.
Client: Sorry, give me a second — I'm getting a little choked up, this whole thing is just exhausting to keep dealing with.
Therapist: Take your time. It makes complete sense to be exhausted by this — you've been managing it for a long time.
Client: Yeah. I turn thirty-five this June and I just thought by now I'd have this figured out.
Therapist: A lot of people carry that exact thought, that there's a deadline on managing anxiety. There isn't one. Let's use today to add a tool to what you already have. I want to walk through the physiological sigh with you — it's different from the box breathing we tried before.
Client: Okay, I'm willing to try anything at this point.
Therapist: Two quick inhales through the nose, one right after the other, then one long slow exhale through the mouth. Let's do it together, three rounds.
Client: Okay... that actually is different, my shoulders dropped a bit after the second round.
Therapist: That physical shift is exactly what we're going for. This one activates the vagus nerve a bit more directly than slow breathing alone, so it can work faster in an acute moment, like the couch on Tuesday.
Client: I like that it's fast. Twenty minutes waiting it out is rough when I'm supposed to be on a call five minutes later.
Therapist: Right, that's the goal — something you can do in under a minute, even mid-meeting if you need to. I'd like you to practice it twice a day this week even when you're not anxious, so it's automatic by the time you actually need it.
Client: I can set a reminder for that. Morning and after lunch probably.
Therapist: That works well. I want to also ask about your sleep this week, since you mentioned before that poor sleep tends to make the physical symptoms worse.
Client: It's been pretty bad, actually. I'm up until midnight or later most nights on my phone, and then I'm exhausted the next day, which probably doesn't help with the anxiety either.
Therapist: That tracks with what we know — poor sleep lowers your threshold for anxiety symptoms, so a rough sleep week and a rough symptom week often go hand in hand. What does the hour or so before bed usually look like for you right now?
Client: Honestly, just scrolling on the couch until I'm tired enough to fall asleep in bed. No real routine to it.
Therapist: Would you be open to trying a more structured wind-down routine this week, alongside the breathing practice?
Client: Yeah, I think I need some structure there, because right now it's pretty chaotic.
Therapist: Let's keep it simple — phone charging outside the bedroom by ten-thirty, and ten minutes of something calming beforehand, like reading or the breathing exercise we just practiced. Does that feel doable?
Client: I think so. The phone outside the room is going to be the hard part, but I know that's probably the part that matters most.
Therapist: It usually is the hardest and most impactful piece, so that's a fair thing to notice. I also want to loop back to Dr. Alvarez's note — she mentioned she's open to a referral to Dr. Renee Okafor, the psychiatrist two floors down from her office, if the physical symptoms don't ease up over the next month. How do you feel about that as a backup plan?
Client: Honestly, kind of relieved there's a next step if this doesn't work. My number's the same if you need to send anything over, 555-0148.
Therapist: I'll note that. Let's plan to check in again in two weeks specifically about the chest tightness frequency and the sleep routine, and if it's not trending down we'll go ahead and make that referral together.
Client: That sounds fair. Thanks, this helped today.
Therapist: Of course, Marcus. Take care of yourself this week.
